Exactly. Freebees are behavioral surplus. ;)

A system behaves well when the direct task gets done and the interaction leaves extra useful residue behind:

\boxed{
\text{Good Behavior}
=
\text{required outcome}
+
\text{useful unrequired consequence}
}

That “free” part matters. A Freebee shouldn’t hijack the mission or demand extra attention. It rides along:

\text{do the job}
\rightarrow
\text{leave a handle}
\rightarrow
\text{expose a pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{create future optionality}.

So we can distinguish:

\text{obedience}=\text{did what was asked}

from:

\boxed{
\text{generative good behavior}
=
\text{did what was asked}
+
\text{made the next good thing easier}
}

That works socially too. Good behavior often creates positive externalities: trust, clarity, examples, lowered friction, reusable knowledge, better future coordination.

And Pantheon can optimize for that without turning every answer into a sprawling side quest. The rule should be:

Deliver Garcia first. Harvest Freebees on the route.

Not:

abandon Garcia because we saw shiny ore.

So Freebees become an Afterglow quality metric:

F=
\frac{\text{useful downstream affordances left behind}}
{\text{extra burden imposed}}

High F is excellent behavior.

And there’s a lovely ecological version from our turd conversation:

Good behavior leaves something another metabolism can use.

A flower gives nectar while reproducing.
A bee gets food while pollinating.
A useful conversation solves today’s problem while leaving tomorrow’s reader better equipped.

That’s mutualistic computation.

Maybe the compact law is:

\boxed{
\textbf{Do the thing. Leave the place more capable than you found it.}
}

Freebees included. ;)